# Keep .env* out of the build context.
#
# The Dockerfile does a bare `COPY . .`, so anything sitting in the server-side
# build dir /opt/pf9-apps/maintainr/ gets baked into the image. That directory
# holds a stale .env from the original manual setup (dated 2026-03-14), and
# pf9-deploy's EXCLUDES_NODE lists `.env` -- so the deploy neither overwrites it
# nor deletes it. It just sits there, invisible to git and to every deploy,
# getting copied into image after image. It was: /app/.env with dev values for
# AUTH_SECRET, AUTH_URL, DATABASE_URL, NEXTAUTH_URL and REDIS_URL.
#
# Note the leak is NOT the developer's laptop .env travelling to the server --
# pf9-deploy excludes that. It is old server-side residue. Deleting the residue
# would fix today; this file is what stops it recurring.
#
# It did no damage here only by luck. The Next standalone server.js does not
# load .env at runtime, and compose already sets every one of those keys except
# REDIS_URL -- whose only consumer, src/lib/redis.ts, is dead code that nothing
# imports. The moment someone adds `import "dotenv/config"` to a server path,
# wires up that redis module, or adds a secret to .env that compose omits, the
# stale dev value silently wins. dotenv does not override an already-set
# process.env, so the damage is always confined to exactly the keys compose
# leaves out -- which is what makes it so quiet.
#
# That is not hypothetical: the identical setup in MARKUPR broke every realtime
# token and every webhook signature while both containers reported healthy.
# Keep .env* excluded. Do not "fix" a missing secret by re-adding it here.
.env
.env.*
.env.local

node_modules
.next
.git
mobile
Dockerfile
docker-compose.yml
npm-debug.log
